aspnetcore,https://github.com/agentic-review-benchmarks/aspnetcore/pull/2,Disposal order causing potential race condition,"The connection disposal order is reversed - disposing secondConnection before the connection that is still being used. In the test, 'connection' performs an InvokeAsync call and its TaskCompletionSource (tcs) is awaited immediately before disposal. However, disposing secondConnection first may cause premature cleanup of shared resources or message handlers since both connections share the same user ('*'). This can lead to flaky test behavior where the connection's pending operations are interrupted by the disposal of secondConnection, especially in distributed scenarios where both connections interact with the same Redis backplane.",src/SignalR/server/StackExchangeRedis/test/RedisEndToEnd.cs,210.0,214.0,"await connection.InvokeAsync(""EchoUser"", ""*"", ""Hello, World!"").DefaultTimeout();
Assert.Equal(""Hello, World!"", await tcs.Task.DefaultTimeout());

await secondConnection.DisposeAsync().DefaultTimeout();
await connection.DisposeAsync().DefaultTimeout();",
aspnetcore,https://github.com/agentic-review-benchmarks/aspnetcore/pull/2,Race condition from premature TaskCompletionSource recreation,"The _receiveTcs TaskCompletionSource is recreated before checking if the buffer is zero-sized, creating a race condition in the WebSocketWrapper's ReceiveAsync implementation. When a zero-byte read occurs (buffer.Count == 0), the method returns early with the result but the TCS has already been reset. This means if another thread or the background task tries to set a result on the now-recreated TCS while this zero-byte read is in progress, it will affect the next receive operation rather than completing the current one. This breaks the synchronization contract and can cause message loss or out-of-order receives in the stateful reconnection test scenarios where WebSocket message sequencing is critical.",src/SignalR/server/StackExchangeRedis/test/RedisEndToEnd.cs,406.0,417.0,"public override async Task<WebSocketReceiveResult> ReceiveAsync(ArraySegment<byte> buffer, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    var res = await _receiveTcs.Task;
    _receiveTcs = new(TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously);
    // Handle zero-byte reads
    if (buffer.Count == 0)
    {
        return res.Item1;
    }",
