dify,https://github.com/agentic-review-benchmarks/dify/pull/3,Incorrect code_executor parameter breaks dependency injection chain,"In the DifyNodeFactory constructor, the template renderer initialization passes the `code_executor` parameter instead of `self._code_executor`. Since `code_executor` is the optional constructor parameter that may be None, while `self._code_executor` is the resolved non-None instance (with fallback to CodeExecutor class), this breaks the dependency injection pattern. When users don't provide a code_executor, the renderer receives None instead of the default CodeExecutor, causing template rendering to fail with AttributeError when attempting to call `execute_workflow_code_template` on None.",api/core/workflow/nodes/node_factory.py,63.0,63.0,        self._template_renderer = template_renderer or CodeExecutorJinja2TemplateRenderer(code_executor),
dify,https://github.com/agentic-review-benchmarks/dify/pull/3,Variable scope error causes UnboundLocalError when template rendering succeeds,"The length validation check was moved inside the try block before the except clause, creating a critical variable scope issue. When `render_template()` succeeds, the `rendered` variable is assigned inside the try block. However, if the length check passes and no exception occurs, execution continues to the return statement outside the try-except block which references `rendered`. In Python, this creates an undefined variable scenario where `rendered` is only defined within the try block scope but accessed outside it, resulting in UnboundLocalError at runtime whenever template rendering succeeds without exceeding length limits.",api/core/workflow/nodes/template_transform/template_transform_node.py,66.0,80.0,"        try:
            rendered = self._template_renderer.render_template(self.node_data.template, variables)
